David","color":"#B07CD4"},{"label":"New covenant","color":"#E4DFCE"},{"label":"Unassigned","color":"#5A6178"}],"relkinds":[{"key":"quoted-in","label":"Quoted in","color":"#E8C15A","strong":true},{"key":"fulfilled-in","label":"Fulfilled in","color":"#D9715C","strong":true},{"key":"parallels","label":"Parallels","color":"#6E9BD1","strong":false},{"key":"illustrates-doctrine","label":"Illustrates doctrine","color":"#9B8AD1","strong":false},{"key":"part-of-covenant","label":"Part of covenant","color":"#5FBFA0","strong":false}],"counts":{"topics":4,"portals":0,"edges":3,"relations":0,"outbound":0},"nodes":[{"id":"bt_book_nam","p":0,"x":56,"y":109,"s":-1,"g":6,"bk":"NAM","ty":"BOOK_OVERVIEW","t":"Nahum: overview","d":"The burden of Nineveh, the vision of Nahum the Elkoshite: the LORD is both a jealous avenger and a stronghold to those who trust him, and Nineveh's fall is described and explained.","q":"Can {{name}} say what Nahum is about and describe the LORD as he is shown in chapter 1?","ev":["Say what the book's heading names (1:1)","Outline: the LORD's character (1), the city's fall (2), the reasons and a final lament (3)"],"pp":[[33,1,1,15],[33,2,1,13],[33,3,1,19]],"tier":null},{"id":"bt_nam_lord_jealous_and_good","p":0,"x":56,"y":239,"s":0,"g":6,"bk":"NAM","ty":"THEME","t":"The LORD: jealous, slow to anger, a stronghold","d":"Nahum describes the LORD as jealous and avenging yet slow to anger, who will not acquit the wicked, before whom mountains quake, and who is a strong hold to those who trust him; he promises to break the yoke from Judah and cut off Nineveh's idols.","q":"Can {{name}} describe how Nahum 1 shows the LORD as both a judge and a stronghold?","ev":["Read 1:2-3 and 1:7 and describe what they say about the LORD","Describe the good news to Judah in 1:12-15"],"pp":[[33,1,1,15]],"tier":null},{"id":"bt_nam_nineveh_falls","p":0,"x":56,"y":369,"s":1,"g":6,"bk":"NAM","ty":"STORY","t":"The attack on Nineveh","d":"Nahum describes the assault on the city, with chariots, flooded gates and fleeing defenders, and its plunder, until the LORD of hosts declares himself against Nineveh and its lion's den.","q":"Can {{name}} describe how Nahum 2 pictures the fall of Nineveh and who is said to be against her?","ev":["Describe the attack scenes in 2:3-10","Read the LORD's declaration in 2:13"],"pp":[[33,2,1,13]],"tier":null},{"id":"bt_nam_woe_bloody_city","p":0,"x":244,"y":369,"s":1,"g":6,"bk":"NAM","ty":"THEME","t":"Woe to the bloody city","d":"Nahum pronounces woe on a city full of lies and blood and compares it with No, which also fell; its strongholds, merchants and captains will fail, and no healing remains for a wound that all who hear of her applaud.","q":"Can {{name}} explain why Nahum 3 compares Nineveh with No and how the book ends?","ev":["Name the sins of the city in 3:1 and 3:4","Say what happened to No and why it is mentioned (3:8-10)","Read how the book ends (3:19)"],"pp":[[33,3,1,19]],"tier":null}],"edges":[[1,0,0],[2,1,1],[3,2,0]],"rel":[]}
